{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 6, :comments ["<b>You have dwelt long enough at this mountain; turn and journey for yourselves.</b> This is the first reproof — that the people hated the Land and settled on this mountain in permanent habitation, and did not turn their faces toward the Land, the place designated for the fulfillment of the commandments. And 'רַב לָכֶם' is in the language of 'רַב לָכֶם בְּנֵי לֵוִי' (Numbers 16:7) — that is, is this rebellion and this trespass a small thing? For indeed it is much — 'רַב' — for you, this matter of permanent settlement on this mountain. Turn, direct your faces toward the Land, and journey for yourselves to your very own source, for from there the material of the first man was fashioned, in the manner that He said to Abraham 'לֶךְ לְךָ.' And further on he gave an explicit exposition on this reproof and said: 'וַנִּסַּע מֵחֹרֵב וַנֵּלֶךְ אֵת כָּל הַמִּדְבָּר' etc., and it should have said 'וַנִּפֶן וַנִּסַּע מֵחֹרֵב,' in the manner that He said 'פְּנוּ וּסְעוּ' — but thus He said to them: You still persist in your impulsiveness, for I said 'פְּנוּ וּסְעוּ' — that when you depart from Mount Horev, the place where you learned Torah, you should turn your faces toward the Land, the place of keeping the Torah, for the study is not the main thing but the practice. And you, when you departed from Horev, a different spirit was with you — to journey from a place designated for Torah study, and toward the place of keeping the commandments you did not turn your faces, and in your rebellion you stand hating the Land. Therefore it says: 'וַנִּסַּע מֵחֹרֵב וַנֵּלֶךְ אֵת כָּל הַמִּדְבָּר' — each one of you set his face toward the wilderness and not toward the Land, since after all the spies said 'נָשׁוּבָה מִצְרָיְמָה' (Numbers 14:4); but after the deed of the spies, when He foretold for them a great punishment, they repented — not of their own goodwill — and there it says: 'וַנִּפֶן וַנִּסַּע' (Deuteronomy 2:1)." "And some say that He held them to blame because they fled from Mount Horev like a child fleeing from school, and concerning this He said 'רַב לָכֶם' — for the days that you dwelt on the mountain were not in your eyes, in your love for them, as but a few days, but as many days of pain. And this is 'רַב לָכֶם' — it was much and exceedingly much for you, this dwelling on this mountain — for you, and not for Me. And it appears that one may settle by this what is said: 'בָּאתֶם עַד הַר הָאֱמֹרִי אֲשֶׁר ה׳ אֱלֹהֵינוּ נֹתֵן לָנוּ' (Deuteronomy 1:20) — since it says 'לָנוּ,' it should have said 'בָּאנוּ עַד הַר הָאֱמֹרִי'; but thus He said: You came as far as the mountain of the Amorite, but not I, for you removed your mind and your thoughts entirely from Mount Horev — but I, my thought constantly wanders there, as though I am still there, for that glorious station is always before My eyes."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 1, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:11:09.089836398Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}